# # text = """The training and inference processes of frontier large language models (LLMs) now require unprecedented resources, with the training of OpenAI's GPT-4 model using 25,000 NVIDIA A100 GPUs and inference using multiple clusters of 128 GPUs distributed across different datacenters. Although the training of these models is resource intensive with high upfront costs, the inference phase is continuous, with successful AI applications generating costs often exceeding training due to cumulative predictions. Distributed deployment has become a requirement for serving large-scale LLMs.

# # Most modern LLMs are transformer-based models equipped with attention mechanisms that ensure the model has the language context to accurately determine input meaning and predict output. To distribute models across GPUs and machines, parallelization strategies such as data parallelism, tensor parallelism, and pipeline parallelism are used, distributing training or inference workloads in various dimensions across the model and data. These parallelization methods allow for the scaling we see in contemporary models, but require frequent communication between GPUs to exchange parameters, activations, and intermediate calculations. Collective communication algorithms such as AllReduce and point-to-point transfers move data across GPUs and machines carried by hardware interconnects, including NVLink, PCIe, and Ethernet-based networking. The characteristics of the network impact the performance of an LLM by introducing communication delays and unexpected bottlenecks.

# # Existing research understandably focuses on optimizing distributed training, where communication costs are inflated due to large batches and long training runs. Optimization of these models is widely explored, all in efforts to hide the inherent communication latency present in distributed systems. However, distributed inference has received significantly less attention, despite it directly affecting user experience, cost of operation, and system design. Training and inference are model processes with distinct compute and communication patterns, so they behave differently in response to network latency. Specific inference processes, GPU compute capability, and interconnect technology complicate performance characteristics even further. Investigating how network latency and interconnect quality influence distributed LLM inference can better inform system design strategies for large-scale AI systems.

# # Large language models are transformer-based models that use attention mechanisms in order to make accurate predictions with natural language input. During inference, input text is broken down into fragments named tokens and mapped to vector embeddings. Each token gets projected into query (Q), key (K), and value (V) vectors used to compute attention weights. In the prefill phase, all K and V vectors for input tokens are computed and stored in the key-value (KV) cache. Following prefill is the decode phase, consisting of autoregressive generation of output tokens, where attention is computed for each new token and appended to the KV cache. Prior work has shown that prefill is generally compute bound, through the processing and computations of Q, K and V vectors, while decode is often memory-bound due to its autoregressive nature, requiring the loading of all model weights and the full KV cache at every step. The inherent differences between the two phases result in distinct behavior when deployed in distributed systems.

# # Parallelization strategies, such as data, tensor, and pipeline parallelism, allow LLMs to run across multiple GPUs and multiple nodes by distributing computations across available hardware. Data parallelism replicates model weights and splits data across available GPUs. While a crucial step in distributed training, it is less relevant in distributed inference due to the lower batch sizes. Tensor parallelism divides parameter tensors of each layer across multiple GPUs, such that each GPU has a partition of the model weights. Using AllReduce operations allows for synchronization of intermediate activations across GPUs. As AllReduce communication has been shown to consume a significant portion of iteration time in distributed workloads, tensor parallelism is usually restricted to GPUs within a single node, where high-bandwidth interconnects such as NVLink can prevent significant performance degradation. Pipeline parallelism distributes a model by allocating groups of layers across devices with activations forwarded between stages using point-to-point transfers. Unlike tensor parallelism, which requires frequent communication between GPUs, pipeline parallelism only communicates between stages, making it a good alternative to tensor parallelism when dealing with lower-bandwidth interconnects such as Ethernet. """
